.TH PBUSD 8 "2026-10-25" "pbus @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pbusd \- pbus event dispatcher daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pbusd
.RB [ \-\-foreground ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-level
.IR level ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pbusd
reads events from the named pipe
.IR /run/pbus/bus ,
checks the key presented, then asynchronously launches the commands registered
in
.IR /etc/pbus/listeners.d .
It is normally started at boot by the
.B pbus.service
unit.
.PP
Event and listener declarations are re-read on every incoming event, so an
edit made by hand applies immediately.
.B SIGHUP
re-reads
.I /etc/pbus/pbus.conf
only.
.SH PROTOCOL
An emission is a single line, fields separated by tabs:
.PP
.RS
PBUS2 <event> <key> <nonce> <timestamp> <reply wanted> <base64 payload>
.RE
.PP
Writes under 4096 bytes to a pipe are atomic, which makes concurrent emissions
safe. Beyond that, the emitter drops the payload into
.I /run/pbus/spool
and sends only the
.B @spool
marker.
.PP
When the sixth field is 1, the daemon drops its verdict into
.IR /run/pbus/reply/<nonce> ,
written under a temporary name and renamed, so the emitter never reads half a
status. The verdict is written before the listeners are launched. Stale
replies are swept after five minutes, as abandoned spool files are after an
hour.
.SH SECURITY
The pipe is created mode
.BR 0622 :
any user may emit, nobody but the daemon may read the traffic. The key
therefore travels in the clear through a channel only the daemon reads; on
disk, only a salted SHA\-256 hash is kept, mode
.BR 0600 .
Comparison is constant-time.
.PP
Triggered commands run as root unless the listener sets
.IR user ,
in which case the daemon drops privileges with
.BR setpriv (1).
A listener is therefore as dangerous as a line in root's crontab: grant write
access to
.I /etc/pbus/listeners.d
to administrators only.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B log_level
debug, info, warn or error.
.TP
.B log_file
Path of the log.
.TP
.B max_jobs
Maximum number of concurrent commands; beyond it, the daemon waits.
.TP
.B default_timeout
Default maximum run time of a command, in seconds.
.SH FILES
.I /run/pbus/bus
,
.I /run/pbus/spool
,
.I /run/pbus/reply
,
.I /run/pbus/pbusd.pid
,
.I /run/pbus/stats
,
.I /var/log/pbus/pbus.log
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pbus (1),
.BR setpriv (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
